===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[explain]] [[meticulously]] or in great [[detail#Noun|detail]].
#: {{synonyms|en|analyze|elucidate|explicitate}}
#: {{ux|en|My homework is to '''explicate''' a poem.}}
#* {{RQ:Taylor Holy Living|chapter=Of Christian Religion|section=VII (Of Prayer)|page=284|passage=It is an act of grace and higheſt honour that we duſt and aſhes are admitted to ſpeak to the Eternal God, to run to him as to a Father, to lay open our wants, to complain of our burdens, to '''explicate''' our ſcruples, to beg remedy & eaſe, ſupport and counſel, health and ſafety, deliverance & ſalvation: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Susan Sontag|title=Styles of Radical Will|publisher=Penguin Modern Classics|year=1969|year_published=2009|isbn=978-0-141-97649-5|chapter=Persona|passage=Alternatively, there is the possibility of an extended narration composed of events that are not (wholly) '''explicated''' but are, nevertheless, possible and may even have taken place.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Lipstick Traces|author=w:Greil Marcus|year_published=2009|year=1989|publisher=Faber & Faber|chapter=The Crash of Yesterday's Art|passage=Isou himself, enemy of all conventional discourse, wrote hundreds, then thousands of pages '''explicating''' his theories; {{...|the productions of his followers, treatises and tracts on every kind of art filled with every kind of graph, chart and equation, matched the master's scholastic concentration and his cabalistic hermeticism–if not his endurance.}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michiko Kakutani|title=The Plot Thins, or Are No Stories New?|work=The New York Times|date=2005-04-15|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/04/15/books/the-plot-thins-or-are-no-stories-new.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=In laying out these archetypes, Mr. Booker {{...|— a British newspaper columnist and the founding editor of the satirical magazine Private Eye —}} does a nimble job of collating dozens of stories, using the 34 years he says it took him to write this volume to identify and '''explicate''' all sorts of parallels and analogies that might not occur to the casual reader.}}

